Q: Flavored types (optional symbol property intersection) lose discrimination when base type is a complex template literal A: <p>This is a confirmed TypeScript bug (<a href="https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/63420" rel="nofollow noreferrer">microsoft/TypeScript#63420</a>). The short explanation is that the flavored pattern breaks when your base type is a template literal with a union in the placeholder.</p>
<p>When you write <code>+${CountryCode}${number}${number}</code> and <code>CountryCode</code> is <code>1 | 33 | 44</code>, TypeScript distributes that union and expands <code>PhoneNumber</code> into a union of three concrete template literals internally. Once that happens, the optional flavor property gets distributed across the union members too, and TypeScript's assignability check loses track of the flavor discrimination. That is why <code>Relay</code> becomes assignable to <code>Whistleblower</code> when it should not be.</p>
<p><code>SimplePhone</code> works because <code>+${number}</code> uses an infinite primitive in the placeholder, so TypeScript leaves it as a single template literal type with no distribution. No distribution means the flavor check stays intact.</p>
<p>The fix depends on how much safety you need.</p>
<p><strong>Option 1: switch to a required brand property (most reliable)</strong></p>
<p>typescript</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const BRAND = Symbol(&quot;brand&quot;);
type BrandedWhistleblower = PhoneNumber &amp; { [BRAND]: &quot;whistleblower&quot; };
type BrandedRelay = PhoneNumber &amp; { [BRAND]: &quot;relay&quot; };

function asWhistleblower(p: PhoneNumber): BrandedWhistleblower {
  return p as BrandedWhistleblower;
}
</code></pre>
<p>A required property creates hard structural incompatibility that TypeScript cannot accidentally simplify away. This is the approach I would use for anything security sensitive.</p>
<p><strong>Option 2: avoid the union in the template literal</strong></p>
<p>typescript</p>
<pre><code>type PhoneNumber = `+${number}`;
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps <code>PhoneNumber</code> as a single template literal with no distribution, so flavoring works correctly. The trade-off is you lose compile-time enforcement of the specific country codes.</p>
<p>For anything where mixing up types has real consequences, go with the required brand. The optional flavor pattern is convenient but it relies on a subtle structural check that can break in exactly this kind of edge case.</p>

Q: How to enable partial text selection in SwiftUI like iOS 26 iMessage A: <p>You need to calculate measured width based on its content.</p>
<pre><code>func updateUIView(_ uiView: UITextView, context: Context) {
        uiView.text = text
        uiView.textColor = textColor

        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            let measured = uiView.sizeThatFits(
                CGSize(width: maxWidth, height: .greatestFiniteMagnitude)
            )

            // Clamp width to content, not max
            let finalWidth = min(measured.width, maxWidth)

            if size != CGSize(width: finalWidth, height: measured.height) {
                size = CGSize(width: finalWidth, height: measured.height)
            }
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Here is the sample example</p>
<pre><code>struct TextSelectionView: UIViewRepresentable {
    let text: String
    let textColor: UIColor
    let maxWidth: CGFloat
    @Binding var size: CGSize

    func makeUIView(context: Context) -&gt; UITextView {
        let tv = UITextView()
        tv.isEditable = false
        tv.isSelectable = true
        tv.isScrollEnabled = false
        tv.backgroundColor = .clear
        tv.textContainerInset = .zero
        tv.textContainer.lineFragmentPadding = 0
        tv.font = .systemFont(ofSize: 17)
        tv.textColor = textColor
        return tv
    }

    func updateUIView(_ uiView: UITextView, context: Context) {
        uiView.text = text
        uiView.textColor = textColor

        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            let measured = uiView.sizeThatFits(
                CGSize(width: maxWidth, height: .greatestFiniteMagnitude)
            )

            // Clamp width to content, not max
            let finalWidth = min(measured.width, maxWidth)

            if size != CGSize(width: finalWidth, height: measured.height) {
                size = CGSize(width: finalWidth, height: measured.height)
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Chat Bubble code:-</p>
<pre><code>struct ChatBubble: View {
    let message: String

    @State private var textSize: CGSize = .zero
    private let maxBubbleWidth = UIScreen.main.bounds.width * 0.7

    var body: some View {
        HStack {
            TextSelectionView(
                text: message,
                textColor: .black,
                maxWidth: maxBubbleWidth,
                size: $textSize
            )
            .frame(width: textSize.width, height: textSize.height)
            .padding(12)
            .background(Color.gray.opacity(0.2))
            .cornerRadius(18)

            Spacer()
        }
        .padding(.horizontal)
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: React material UI close when the value selected - multi select A: <p>Hi you can control props <code>open</code>, <code>onOpen</code>, <code>onClose</code>  using state like this :</p>
<pre><code>import { useState, useRef } from &quot;react&quot;;
import { Select, Input, MenuItem } from &quot;@mui/material&quot;;
import &quot;./styles.css&quot;;

export default function App() {
  const [personName, setPersonName] = useState([]);
  const names = [&quot;john&quot;, &quot;josh&quot;];
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);

  const handleChange = (e) =&gt; {
    setPersonName(e.target.value);
    setOpen(false);
  };

  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;App&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Select
        open={open}
        onOpen={() =&gt; setOpen(true)}
        onClose={() =&gt; setOpen(false)}
        multiple
        value={personName}
        onChange={handleChange}
        input={&lt;Input /&gt;}
      &gt;
        &lt;MenuItem disabled value=&quot;&quot;&gt;
          &lt;em&gt;Placeholder&lt;/em&gt;
        &lt;/MenuItem&gt;
        {names.map((name) =&gt; (
          &lt;MenuItem key={name} value={name}&gt;
            {name}
          &lt;/MenuItem&gt;
        ))}
      &lt;/Select&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
